.page patchouli 1 the manual viewer (Patchouli's library)
.alias man

# SYNOPSIS
*patchouli* [*-atrW* _N_] [_SECTION_] _NAME_...
*patchouli* *-k* _WORD_...
*patchouli* *-f* _NAME_...
*patchouli* *-l*
*patchouli* *-w* _NAME_...
*patchouli* *--check-all*
*patchouli* *--check* _FILE_...

# DESCRIPTION
`patchouli` shows the manual pages that live in Patchouli's library. A page is a short text file in the `.pw` format (see pw(5)); the installed ones are kept under `/ScarletDevilMansion/share/patchouli`, one directory per section. `man` is another name for the same program.

When standard output is a terminal the page is formatted with bold and colour and handed to the pager (see patchouli-pager(1)); `q` leaves it and `h` lists the keys. When output goes to a file or a pipe, or with `-t`, the page is plain text.

The sections are numbered the traditional way: 1 commands, 5 file formats, 7 overviews and conventions, 8 system administration. Without a section number the search goes through 1, 8, 5 and 7 in that order and the first page found is shown.

# OPTIONS
@ *-a*, *--all*
    Show the page from every section that has one of that name.
@ *-t*, *--text*
    Plain text: no colours, no pager.
@ *-W* _N_, *--width*=_N_
    Make the lines at most _N_ columns long. The default is the terminal width, but never more than 78.
@ *-r*, *--raw*
    Print the page source instead of the formatted page.
@ *-k*, *--apropos*
    Search the names and one-line descriptions of all pages for each _WORD_ (ignoring case) and list the matches.
@ *-f*, *--whatis*
    List the one-line description of the pages called _NAME_.
@ *-l*, *--list*
    List every page in the library, one per line.
@ *-w*, *--where*
    Print the file a page lives in instead of showing it.
@ *--check-all*
    Parse and render every page and report mistakes in them: unknown directives, missing sections, references to pages that do not exist. The exit status is 1 if anything was found.
@ *--check* _FILE_...
    The same for particular files.
@ *-h*, *--help*
    Print a short usage message.
@ *-V*, *--version*
    Print the version.

An argument that contains a slash or ends in `.pw` is taken to be a file and is formatted directly, which is handy while writing a page.

# ENVIRONMENT
@ PAGER
    The pager to use on a terminal. The default is `patchouli-pager`. An empty value or `cat` disables paging.
@ PATCHOULI_PATH
    A colon separated list of directories to search instead of the installed library. Each holds one directory per section.
@ MANWIDTH
    Line length to use instead of the terminal width.
@ COLUMNS
    The terminal width, if it cannot be asked of the console.

# EXAMPLES
    patchouli ls
    patchouli 7 regex
    patchouli -k directory
    patchouli -l | grep '^ps'
    patchouli --check-all

# EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if a page was not found or a check failed; 2 for a usage error.

# SEE ALSO
patchouli-pager(1), pw(5), gensokyo(7)

Patchouli would like you to know that she has read every page in here, and that she sneezes when it is dusty.
